{"id": "q0114", "pool": "frozen", "arm": "local", "tier": "never", "lang": "en", "run_id": "ttfa1", "attempt": 1, "seed": 3033147943, "protocol": "ttfa-v3", "config_sha256": "923cbbdfe3213927dfa36b1f8e0eb48a30d641d9b9bf7cf25d16765340d88836", "gate_art_sha256": "0e6494c2eeac9bcd86c10b5def3cbd32e98bb0765fa2fd8afc8c1b47915ea372", "threshold": null, "gpu": "NVIDIA H100 80GB HBM3", "host": "modal", "shard": 1, "query": "When converting $\\frac{31}{11111}$ to a decimal, the decimal turns out to be a repeating decimal. How many digits repeat in this repeating decimal?\n\nFor example, if you get the repeating decimal $0.\\overline{123},$ then your answer should be $3,$ and if you get $0.436\\overline{7},$ your answer should be $1.$\n\nSolve and give the final answer.", "input_path": "/data/audio_pool/q0114.wav", "input_sha256": "1aac0c2181a56089a0f15195816626de48285ef19be54fcca2f7a7a976ccc4e8", "input_samples": 384600, "input_s": 24.038, "n_real_chunks": 25, "score": 0.8536, "act": 0.9978, "is_info": true, "fired": false, "mode": "local", "answer": "Okay, so we need to convert the fraction 3 1 over 11 1 to a decimal and see if it repeat s. Let me do the division. 1 11 goes into 31 zero times, so we add a decimal point and a zero, making it 3 10. 1 1 1 times 2 is 2 22, subtract that from 3 10, I get 88. Bring down another zero to make 88 0. 1 1 1 goes into 8 80 seven times because 7 times 11 1 is 7 7 7. Sub tract that, I get 10 3. B ring down a zero, making 1 030. 1 1 1 goes into 1 030 nine times, since 9 times 11 1 is 9 99. Sub tract that, I get 31. Wait, that's the same remainder as before! So now the pattern will repeat. The decimal starts as 0.2 809... and then it repeats 28 09. Looking at the repeating part, it' s four digits long. So the answer is 4.", "relay_text": "", "expert_answer": "", "transcript": "", "asr_s": null, "expert_s": null, "expert_input_samples": null, "expert_input_s": null, "expert_input_sha256": null, "expert_timed_out": false, "wait_s": null, "rel