{"id": "q0144", "pool": "frozen", "arm": "local", "tier": "never", "lang": "en", "run_id": "ttfa1", "attempt": 1, "seed": 3384663650, "protocol": "ttfa-v3", "config_sha256": "923cbbdfe3213927dfa36b1f8e0eb48a30d641d9b9bf7cf25d16765340d88836", "gate_art_sha256": "0e6494c2eeac9bcd86c10b5def3cbd32e98bb0765fa2fd8afc8c1b47915ea372", "threshold": null, "gpu": "NVIDIA H100 80GB HBM3", "host": "modal", "shard": 1, "query": "Suppose the polynomial\n$$f(x) = a_nx^n + a_{n-1}x^{n-1} + \\cdots + a_2x^2 + a_1x + a_0$$has integer coefficients, and its roots are distinct integers.\n\nGiven that $a_n=2$ and $a_0=66$, what is the least possible value of $|a_{n-1}|$?\n\nSolve and give the final answer.", "input_path": "/data/audio_pool/q0144.wav", "input_sha256": "e888e57e51e9a25af9496b85657e51995827d2223b1d1c8c837f5e6ae9c64986", "input_samples": 289000, "input_s": 18.062, "n_real_chunks": 19, "score": 0.9156, "act": 0.9988, "is_info": true, "fired": false, "mode": "local", "answer": "So we have a polynom ial with integer coefficient s, and its roots are distinct integers. That means that the polynomial can be factored into linear factors with integer root s. Since the leading coefficient is two, which is not prime, it could be written as two times another polynomial with integer coefficients. But since all the roots are in tegers, the other factor must also have integer coefficients. Therefore, the leading coefficient of the other factor must be plus or minus one. So let's say the polynomial is two times p of x, where p of x is a polynomial with integer coefficients and integer roots. Now, the constant term of p of x is sixty- six divided by two, which is thirty-three. The constant term of p of x is the product of all the roots of p of x, up to sign. So we need to find distinct integer factors of thirty-three whose product has absolute value thirty three. Th ree and eleven work, but they're too big for n minus one. One and thirty-three work, but again, thirty- three is too big. Three and negative eleven work, but then n would be four, which is less than five. One and negative thirty- three work, but thirty-three is too big. Three and negative thirteen don't work because they're not factors of thirty-three. Wait, actually, I should check if there are any other factor pairs. Let me think. Thirty-three is three times eleven. So the possible distinct factor pairs are one and thirty- three, three and eleven, three and negative eleven, one and negative thirty-th", "relay_text": "", "expert_answer": "", "transcript": "", "asr_s": null, "expert_s": null, "expert_input_samples": null, "expert_input_s": null, "expert_input_sha256": null, "expert_timed_out": false, "wait_s": null, "relay_synth_ms": null, "onset_chunk": 18, "eot_seen": false, "n_speak_chunks": 60, "feed_lag_max_ms": 46, "feed_lag_sum_ms": 46, "audio_files": {"local": {"path": "/data/ttfa_real/ttfa1/frozen/audio/q0144_local_local.wav", "sha256": "d97b2c5b4f2f50f646ca4bdf7f42c98c4b82a6a2947b858f57b42eaa3e08c31e", "sr": 24000, "seconds": 60.0}}, "chunks": [[0, 1, 1.0, 1.059, 1.106, 1, 0, 0], [1, 1, 2.0, 2.06, 2.106, 1, 0, 0], [2, 1, 3.0, 3.054, 3.102, 1, 0, 0], [3, 1, 4.0, 4.059, 4.108, 1, 0, 0], [4, 1, 5.0, 5.058, 5.107, 1, 0, 0], [5, 1, 6.0, 6.059, 6.109, 1, 0, 0], [6, 1, 7.0, 7.06, 7.107, 1, 0, 0], [7, 1, 8.0, 8.058, 8.109, 1, 0, 0], [8, 1, 9.0, 9.056, 9.099, 1, 0, 0], [9, 1, 10.0, 10.061, 10.107, 1, 0, 0], [10, 1, 11.0, 11.056, 11.099, 1, 0, 0], [11, 1, 12.0, 12.064, 12.109, 1, 0, 0], [12, 1, 13.0, 13.058, 13.102, 1, 0, 0], [13, 1, 14.0, 14.064, 14.109, 1, 0, 0], [14, 1, 15.0, 15.058, 15.102, 1, 0, 0], [15, 1, 16.0, 16.065, 16.111, 1, 0, 0], [16, 1, 17.0, 17.058, 17.101, 1, 0, 0], [17, 1, 18.0, 18.064, 18.109, 1, 0, 0], [18, 1, 18.062, 18.164, 18.735, 0, 24000, 0], [19, 0, 19.062, 19.126, 19.713, 0, 24000, 0], [20, 0, 20.062, 20.123, 20.653, 0, 24000, 0], [21, 0, 21.062, 21.122, 21.617, 0, 24000, 0], [22, 0, 22.062, 22.125, 22.624, 0, 24000, 0], [23, 0, 23.062, 23.124, 23.618, 0, 24000, 0